Confirmed: `BindSoleSeat(engine, ...)` does NOT populate the `PlayerEntityLookup` global. The `SeatPossessionSyncSystem` requires that lookup to have the player. The cleanest core-side fix: `BindSoleSeat` should also upsert the player in the engine's PlayerEntityLookup (it's the engine-level "bind this player to this seat/rep" primitive — the lookup is derived state that must follow). This is a legitimate part of the branch's seat API contract (the branch probably populated lookup via map resolution only, but engine-side binding should maintain it too).

Patch ClientLocalSeatBindings.BindSoleSeat (engine overload): after setting seats, upsert PlayerEntityLookup in GlobalContext:

```csharp
if (engine.GlobalContext.TryGetValue(CoreServiceKeys.PlayerEntityLookup.Name, out object? lookupObj) &&
    lookupObj is PlayerEntityLookup existing)
{
    if (!existing.TryGet(playerId, out Entity bound) || !engine.World.IsAlive(bound))
    {
        existing.ReplaceWith(...)? // Register throws if different entity
    }
}
```
Register throws when rebinding to a DIFFERENT entity. Need replace semantics: build fresh lookup with just this player when the existing entry is absent/dead/mismatched. But replacing wholesale drops OTHER players — for sole-seat binding that's the intent (sole!). Use ReplaceWith(new lookup {player}).

Actually — wait, the world property on engine — BindSoleSeat uses engine.GetService — engine.World available. Implement:

```csharp
UpsertSolePlayerLookup(engine, playerId, possessedRep);

private static void UpsertSolePlayerLookup(GameEngine engine, int playerId, Entity possessedRep)
{
    var replacement = new PlayerEntityLookup();
    replacement.Register(playerId, possessedRep);
    if (engine.GlobalContext.TryGetValue(CoreServiceKeys.PlayerEntityLookup.Name, out object? lookupObj) &&
        lookupObj is PlayerEntityLookup existing)
    {
        existing.ReplaceWith(replacement);
    }
    else
    {
        engine.GlobalContext[CoreServiceKeys.PlayerEntityLookup.Name] = replacement;
    }
}
```

This makes ANY engine-side sole-seat binding coherent with the sync system. Also the GameEngine likely stores PlayerEntityLookup as a service too — SetService as well for consistency. Check where lookup gets SET as service: `engine.SetService(CoreServiceKeys.PlayerEntityLookup, ...)`? Earlier ParticipantBindingResolver.PublishLocalSeats(globals...) — the session's lookup gets stashed... MapSession? The sync reads `_globals` — GlobalContext — my upsert covers it.

Apply, rebuild, rerun.

